#heading(numbering: none, outlined: false)[Abstract]

A fair lock pays for fairness because the thread it serves also runs its own critical section: choosing who goes next also chooses where the protected data must move. It therefore either migrates the data on every hand-off or reserves the lock for one thread and lets it sit idle, as u-SCL does with millisecond lock slices. Delegation removes this coupling. We present FC-PQ, a flat-combining lock whose combiner runs every critical section on its own core and always serves the thread with the least accumulated critical-section time, so it is usage-fair, work-conserving, and keeps the data in one cache. In redb, an embedded key-value store with a single-writer lock, FC-PQ raises the Jain index over per-thread critical-section time between 1-record and 64-record writers from 0.85--0.86 under the FIFO MCS queue lock to 0.94--0.995, with 1.17--1.34× MCS's transaction throughput and 7--11× fewer cache-to-cache transfers.
